  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 218 AM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nAn upper-level low will move across the Texas/Oklahoma panhandles\nSaturday night before slowly lifting east into Oklahoma on Sunday.\nRain showers may persist over the eastern Permian Basin late\nSaturday night before moisture and ascent exit to our east by\nSunday morning. A cold front will push through our forecast area\non Sunday behind the upper system. Northerly winds will increase\nbehind the passage of the front Sunday evening. Temperatures also\ntrend cooler Sunday and Sunday night behind this feature with\nhighs in the upper 50s to mid 60s for most of the region (except\n70s over the Big Bend/Lower Trans Pecos) and lows mostly in the\nlower 40s to lower 50s. Our forecast area will remain located\nalong the western periphery of surface high pressure centered over\nthe eastern U.S. early next week. Low level southeasterly to \nsoutherly flow on top of a cool easterly surface flow may keep \nskies mostly cloudy to overcast with low cloud cover across much \nof our region Monday, along with a good signal that highs will \nstay well below normal in the 50s over most of the area. Another \nday of mostly cloudy to overcast skies and below normal \ntemperatures in the lower 50s to lower 60s may continue into \nTuesday. The surface ridge remains positioned over the eastern\nCONUS Wednesday and Thursday. Low level southerly flow may keep\nlow cloud cover in place over eastern portions of our forecast\narea, especially from the Permian Basin into the Lower Trans Pecos\nWednesday and Thursday. Temperatures remain below normal, but may\nmoderate into the lower to mid 60s over much of the region by \nThursday.",
